.TH QRTEST 1
.SH NAME
qrtest \- regression test for qr(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qrtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qrtest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/qr.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(args,\ data) ,
and checks it byte-for-byte against a live oracle - built, unusually
for this backlog, from a
.I patched
copy of the real 9front source rather than the unmodified source
itself. See
.IR qr (1)'s
own BUGS: the real
.B qr.c
has a genuine uninitialized-variable bug
.RB ( encode() 's
own
.BR bc )
that corrupts its own output non-deterministically for QR version 2
and above, so building the unmodified source would fail its own test
runs for reasons that are the source's own accident, not this port's.
.BR buildoracle()
instead reads the real source via
.IR cmd (3)'s
.BR cat ,
applies the single-line
.B "bc = 0;"
fix (isolated during this port's own development by a negative-control
sweep - see the
.B inferno-rio-qr-encoder-bug
memory) as a plain Limbo string substitution, writes the patched copy
back out via
.IR cmd (3)
again, and compiles
.I that
copy with
.B clang
against plan9port's prebuilt
.BR libbio / lib9 ,
same as every other built oracle in this backlog since
.IR urlencodetest (1)
established the pattern. Every other byte of the real source is
untouched.
.PP
Because
.I qr.b
implements the identical algorithm as the (patched) oracle, genuine
byte-for-byte agreement is the right expectation here - unlike
comparing against a
.I different
QR encoder (segno, zxing's own encoder, etc.), which would
legitimately differ in which of the 8 standard mask patterns it picks
without either side being wrong; QR's own spec permits any of the 8.
See
.IR qr (1)'s
own BUGS for the related
.BR evaluate() " oddity."
.SH CASES
.TP
.I t1versions
A range of forced versions (1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 10, 15, 20, 27, 40) across
all four error-correction levels, byte mode - exercises single-block
through many-interleaved-block codeword layouts. Two payload sizes,
not one - see BUGS for why a single size doesn't work at every forced
version.
.TP
.I t2modes
Numeric, alphanumeric, and byte mode (including raw control and
high-bit bytes, to catch anything that assumes printable ASCII).
.TP
.I t3autoversion
No
.B -v
- lets
.BR formsel() 's
own version-search pick the smallest fit, across payload sizes
straddling several version-capacity boundaries, at all four levels.
.TP
.I t4bundledv
Attached
.RB ( -v3 )
vs separate
.RB ( "-v 3" )
version-flag forms, matching
.IR cb (1)'s
own precedent of checking bundled-flag parsing against a real binary
rather than assuming it.
.TP
.I t5toolarge
A payload too large for version 40 even at the lowest-redundancy
level must fail exactly like
.BR qrcode() 's
own nil-return contract - checked against the real oracle's own
non-zero exit status, not just "produces different output".
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/qrtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR qr (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not independently re-verify the
.B bc
finding via an external QR decoder every run - that was established
once, empirically, during this port's own development (a real build
of the unmodified source, decoded with
.BR zxing-cpp ,
a real independent decoder installed into a throwaway Python venv)
and is recorded in
.B doc/plan9-port.md
and the
.B inferno-rio-qr-encoder-bug
memory rather than re-run here: pulling a Python venv and a native
decoder library into this suite's own dependencies would make it
markedly less reproducible than every other oracle in this backlog,
for a finding that does not need re-demonstrating on every run.
.PP
Does not exercise
.IR qr (1)'s
own CLI
.I file
argument path or its 8192-byte
.B readn()
truncation limit - every case here drives
.BR run() 's
own
.I data
byte array directly.
.PP
This suite's own development caught two of its own test-sizing
mistakes, not qr.b bugs, both by getting a real "too many bits"
rejection where none was intended: an early
.I t1versions
draft used one 58-byte message for every forced version, exceeding
version 1's own capacity at every level; the fix (7 bytes) still
required checking
.BR formsel() 's
own arithmetic directly rather than assuming byte-mode "8 data bytes"
means "8 bytes of capacity is enough" - the mode/count/terminator
overhead means an 8-byte payload actually needs 10 bytes of real
capacity, one more than version 1's smallest
.RB ( -H )
level provides. Separately,
.I t3autoversion 's
largest size was lowered from 1500 to 1200 bytes after the same
rejection appeared at version 40's own
.B -H
capacity (1276 data bytes, the smallest of the four levels at the
largest version).
.PP
A real, severe TRANSLATION bug in
.BR qr.b " itself -"
not this test file, but found only by running this suite - is
recorded in
.IR qr (1)'s
own BUGS: an early translation pass of
.BR evaluate() 's
run-length scan added an increment the real C source does not have,
assuming a missing
.B r++
was an oversight in the source rather than the source's own dead
code. This changed which mask
.I qr.b
itself picked as "best" on about half of this suite's own cases,
producing matching side/length but mismatched module content - caught
by this suite's own byte comparison, then isolated to one minimal
case (version 2, level Q) with a standalone diff probe outside this
file, and confirmed with a genuine negative control before being
fixed.
